[2] Frequency describes how many oscillations occur at one position per unit of time.
One describes space and the other describes time, so a short wavelength does not mean fewer oscillations.
    break: {"before":"Water waves also spread this way, so a wave picture of light cannot treat light only as a thin straight line.\nWavelength describes the distance over which a wave repeats in space.","after":"Frequency describes how many oscillations occur at one position per unit of time.\nOne describes space and the other describes time, so a short wavelength does not mean fewer oscillations."}
[3] In the same medium, when the wave speed is the same, a higher frequency means a shorter wavelength.
The source explains that spreading is especially noticeable when the obstacle is comparable in size to the wavelength.
